  "summary": "WIRED's investigation, based on public records requests, reveals that the Alpharetta, Georgia Police Department shares Flock ALPR data with more than 2,000 organizations nationwide and receives from over 1,300, illustrating how Flock's data-sharing network has expanded far beyond typical local police uses. The article details how default settings, the Nationwide lookup tool, and lax administrator oversight have enrolled agencies like Reynoldsburg (OH) and Palo Alto (CA) in nationwide sharing without their knowledge, while California's NCRIC shows a more constrained model under state law.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Data captured by those Flock cameras, WIRED has learned via public records requests, is accessible to more than 2,000 police departments, colleges, airports, and government agencies across the United States.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "The most likely explanation is that the setting was enabled during initial account setup or onboarding",
      "paragraph_idx": 38
    },
    {
      "quote": "Shikada said that Flock quietly \"disabled\" Nationwide lookup for Palo Alto police in October 2024, and for all California law enforcement by March 2025.",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    },
    {
      "quote": "The particular officer can narrow the networks they want to search, but typically they don't. Typically, they search every network they have access to.",
      "paragraph_idx": 46
    }
  ],
